{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass at that time, and Judah went down.</b> It would have been fitting to attach the passage of 'And Joseph was brought down to Egypt' to the passage above, since the entire subject concerns Joseph." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> Scripture placed the passage of Judah next to it to teach you that his brothers brought him down from his greatness when they saw their father's distress. They said to him: 'You said to us, ״Come, let us sell him to the Ishmaelites״ — had you told us to bring him back, we would have listened to you.'" "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> Scripture placed the passage of levirate marriage next to the passage of the sale, in order to attach one gilgul to another gilgul, for the secret of levirate marriage is the secret of gilgul. And it is known that that sin could not be clarified and purified except through the secret of gilgul in the ten slain of the kingdom, for then the trap was broken and the souls escaped, and went up in peace to their Father in heaven. And the sum of the matter: 'ten brothers of Joseph' — these are the ten slain of the kingdom; and Er and Onan, they are Peretz and Zerach; this is what is written (Genesis 46:12): ״וימת ער ואונן בארץ כנען ויהיו בני יהודה״ — 'And Er and Onan died in the land of Canaan, and the sons of Judah were…'. And behold, this is among the allusions of the Torah, and the discerning will understand. As for the fact that the ten slain of the kingdom and the brothers who performed the deed were only nine, since Reuben did not agree with them — one may say that Joseph also sinned, for the brothers' sin involved him too, for he was the cause, when he lorded it over his brothers who were greater and more honored than he, and he provoked them and vaunted himself against them with his dreams; and since he was included in the sin he was included in the punishment, and therefore he too was called to account. Or one may say that Joseph was not called to account at all, but Reuben was called to account — not on account of Joseph's sin, since Reuben did not sin in his sale, but he sinned with Bilhah and was called to account for that; and therefore they were ten, with Reuben included, for the sin he committed in the matter of Bilhah. And this is alluded to in the verse (Deuteronomy 33:6): ״ויהיו מתיו מספר״ — 'and let his men be a number' — for they were ten together with Reuben; and there I shall write, with the help of HaShem, may He be blessed."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:18:35.544889941Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}